  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z in [-2, 2]; steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. Steps diverge for learning rates above 0.8 (2 / 2.5).",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, size 2·sqrt((64−b)/(63·b)) per direction: largest at batch 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Arrows are drawn at true step length (scale 1, bowl units). Orange: the actual next step, against the noisy slope. Grey: the full-data step, against the true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6 (diverged). Ready means no step yet. The trail marks every step; very high points are capped on screen.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed and run number: Reset replays the same sequence; a new run uses the next noise values. The run advances 7.5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}